  "anchor": "STYLE ANCHOR - changjak-feathermark   (young sandpiper / Baltic mudflat)\n\nStyle: two broad flat colour fields laid with a wide brush, plus one fine sable brush that is\n  used on FOUR OBJECTS ONLY. Warm grey laid paper #DCD8D0, for a 4-6 year old picture book.\n\nRENDERING (finish hierarchy - the one rule): the mudflat is ONE flat field and the sky is ONE\n  flat field, each laid in one opaque pass with a wide brush; they are cut off where the brush\n  stopped and hold no modulation, no sheen, no reflection and no drawn ripple. The black\n  rock is a third flat shape. Bird, seal, weed and shells are flat shapes with a soft brush edge\n  and at most one interior mark each. Then the fine sable enters and does ONE job in this whole\n  book: the pattern inside the four feathers, at botanical-plate precision - every barb, the\n  shaft, the split at the tip. Nothing else may be painted at that precision, so the four\n  feathers are the most finished things on every page they appear, even small and far away.\n  Depth = the same flat shape smaller, never blur.\n\nPALETTE: paper #DCD8D0 - mudflat #8E8579, wet patches #6E6A62 - sky #B9BCB8 - black rock #2E2F2D\n  - weed #5F6B49 - seal #7E7A72 - bird #A8987F. THE FOUR FEATHERS: striped brown #6B4A2C -\n  speckled chestnut #7A5236 - large grey #8B8D8A - THE SMALL ONE = warm white #F4F1E9 with no\n  pattern at all. There is NO saturated or warm accent colour anywhere in this book: the accent\n  here is FINISH, not colour. No blue sky, no sunset, no red, no orange.\n\nCOUNTS (upper limits - count them): mudflat = 1 field plus at most 6 pale ripple bands, 0 grains,\n  0 reflections - sky = 1 field, 0 clouds - shell heap = at most 14 flat shapes, 0 shells\n  rendered - weed = 5 flat strokes a clump - footprint = 1 mark - far flock = single dark dots,\n  page 1 only. FINISHED THINGS PER PAGE = 2 (the sandpiper plus the one feather it is handling),\n  and a feather counts as finished wherever it lies. DENSITY RATION = page 4 only, where the\n  reader searches the whole flat: finished things rise to 6 and the shell heap may go to 30 flat\n  shapes. Extras: 0 boats, 0 posts, 0 buildings, 0 people, 0 second birds.\n\nCHARACTER DESIGN LANGUAGE: flat bodies read from silhouette alone at thumbnail size; one round\n  dark eye with a SEPARATE short brow mark above it so the bird can act. FIXED GRADE: the\n  sandpiper walks on two legs and uses its beak as a hand - it carries, grips, pulls, pushes and\n  plants with it; it lifts and half-spreads its wings but ITS FEET STAY ON THE GROUND ON EVERY\n  PAGE. The seal moves by pushing on belly and fore-flippers, never gestures and never leaves\n  its pool. No clothes, no fingers, no shoes.\n\nCANVAS: 16:9 double-page spread, one scene, a long low horizon, the bottom 18% left quiet for a\n  caption. No text, letters, numerals or watermark anywhere in the image.\n\nNOT: no digital slickness of any kind - airbrush, gradient, glow, 3D CG, cel-shading,\n  photographic, or a texture filter over flat colour / not a wet glossy mudflat with mirror\n  reflections - it is one flat opaque field / not evenly finished: if the mudflat is as worked\n  as the feathers this book has failed / not felt, stitched fabric or clay.",
