   "CHARACTER SHEET - HareHeat   (bake FIRST, before any scene)\n\nSame make as the book: a flat fill with a broad reed-pen contour drawn in the OTHER fill colour,\ndry skipping line. Draw this sheet on a SPLIT GROUND - the left half sun ochre #C9903C, the right\nhalf shade blue-green #2C4038 - so both states of the animal are settled at once. No white\nanywhere on this sheet, and no bare paper.\n\nFACE: young hare, long head, small round muzzle. A drawn eye with a SEPARATE pen brow above it -\n  the brows do the acting and must lift, pull together and tilt unevenly. Whiskers 3 strokes a\n  side. The tongue, when it hangs out panting, is CONTOUR ONLY with no colour inside it.\nEARS: 🔴 THE GAUGE, and the only place the accent red is ever used. Two tall ears; when they fold\n  flat sideways the inside faces the reader. Draw the ladder as three heads in a row, same size\n  and same angle, differing only in the flat step inside the ear: pale #E6B7A6 / flushed #D0664A /\n  scarlet #B22B18. A child must be able to name which is hottest with the sheet across a room.\nBODY: long and loose, four heads tall standing, low and stretched when it lies down. Long hind\n  legs, small forepaws, short lifted tail.\n🔴 IN SUN / IN SHADE (draw the same pose twice, once on each half of the split ground): in sun the\n  body is an ochre shape with a blue-green outline and AT MOST 3 marks inside it; in shade it is a\n  blue-green shape with ochre hatching along the flank and haunch. Same drawing, opposite make.\nSHEET: on hind legs with one forepaw thrown up / four legs braced skidding to a stop, forepaws\n  ploughing forward / lying flat on the belly with all four legs stretched out and the ears spread\n  wide sideways / one forepaw pulling its own ear down so the inside faces us / leaping with all\n  four feet off the ground; plus two close-ups - eyes screwed shut with the mouth wide open\n  panting, and eyes wide open having just understood something. Plain split ground, no road.\nSCENE token: HareHeat.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - TortoiseSun   (bake SECOND)\n\nSame make and same reed nib as HareHeat - attach the approved HareHeat sheet so the two are drawn\nby one hand and so the size between them is settled. Split ground again, sun ochre left, shade\nblue-green right. 🔴 NO ACCENT RED ANYWHERE ON THIS ANIMAL, on any page of the book.\n\nFACE: old-looking tortoise, blunt beak-like mouth, heavy lids. A drawn eye with a SEPARATE pen brow\n  above it, but the brows barely move - this animal is calm on every page. Loose folded skin on the\n  neck drawn as 3 or 4 lines.\nBUILD: 🔴 ONE LOW CLOSED OVAL on four short posts, the exact opposite of the hare's loose length.\n  The shell reads as a single dome shape with a plate pattern of 5 or 6 flat facets on it - never\n  more, and the plates are shapes, not texture.\n🔴 SHELL LADDER (two shells side by side, same size, same angle): WET-DULL - hatching laid across\n  the shell so the plate pattern is muddled and hard to count. DRY-CRISP - the hatching gone, so\n  the plates stand out as clean flat shapes with one confident unbroken contour. 🔴 That is the\n  whole difference: no white highlight, no shine, no gloss mark, ever. The reader reads \"warmed\n  up\" as \"you can suddenly count the plates\".\n🔴 SCALE PLATE: the tortoise beside HareHeat at THE SAME DEPTH - the shell is about as long as the\n  hare's body without its ears, and it sits far lower. Four pages put them side by side and\n  perspective must not flip that.\nSHEET: walking with the neck stretched right out and the legs long and straight, body lifted clear\n  of the ground / walking slowly with the neck half out and eyes half shut / stopped with the neck\n  turned to look sideways / pressed flat to the ground with the neck drawn right back inside and\n  the legs folded under; plus one close-up of the head coming out of the shell. Plain split ground.\nSCENE token: TortoiseSun.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - HillSet   (bake THIRD - the stage, used on every page)\n\nThe road, empty of animals, exactly as it sits on all fourteen pages. Two flat fills, reed-pen\nline in the other colour, and no bare paper anywhere.\n\nTHE PLACE: one ochre dirt road climbing from the bottom of the frame to a big tree at the top of\n  the hill, curving as it goes. Dry grass either side as at most 20 short strokes. Cypresses stand\n  apart along the road, each ONE flat blue-green shape with at most 7 notches on its edge, 0\n  needles, 0 trunk lines. A low stone wall and rolling ridges far off as at most 3 wavering lines.\n🔴 THE EDGE IS THE SUBJECT OF THIS SHEET - draw the road four times in a row, same viewpoint,\n  changing only where the sun/shade edge falls:\n  A · EARLY MORNING - long blue-green bands lying right across the ochre road, three of them.\n  B · LATE MORNING - one clean diagonal edge cutting the frame, wide shade on one side.\n  C · NOON - the shade shrunk to the width of a cypress trunk at its foot; the rest all ochre.\n  D · EVENING - wide bands again, alternating, neither colour winning.\n  Every edge is cut clean. 🔴 Put A and C side by side and check that the shade is a SHAPE WITH\n  AN AREA, not a darker mood.\n🔴 SUN HAS NO LINES IN IT: on the ochre, things get a contour and at most 3 marks. On the\n  blue-green, the same things carry ochre hatching. Show one cypress standing half in each so the\n  difference is settled.\nALSO ON THIS SHEET: a hare-belly hollow pressed into the shade earth · a short line of hare prints\n  on the ochre · one cicada on a branch as 4 marks · a single ochre disc for the low evening sun,\n  sitting inside a blue-green ground. 0 lettering, 0 numerals, 0 signs.\nSCENE token: HillSet."
