   "CHARACTER SHEET - CalfSpot   (bake FIRST, before any scene)\n\nFACE: a young black-and-white spotted calf, short blunt muzzle, wide-set eyes. The head is bare\n  paper with two or three ink patches scribbled solid, one of them across an eye. The eye is a\n  drawn ring with a dark centre and a SEPARATE ink brow above it that lifts, knots and tilts.\n  Nostrils 2 short strokes, ear insides 2 strokes each.\nBODY: small, leggy, knock-kneed, four heads long, soft dewlap, small horn buds not yet through.\n  Patches sit as whole shapes, never as speckle; count them - 6 patches on the body, no more, and\n  they keep the same shape and place on every page so the calf is recognisable from behind.\n🔴 THE TAIL LADDER (draw both, same pose, same size, side by side): DOWN - hanging loose and\n  slightly curved, the tuft brushing the hock. UP - the whole tail thrown STRAIGHT UP above the\n  back, stiff as a pole, drawn with ONE overrun stroke that shoots past the tuft. This one mark\n  is the evidence of the whole book and must be the strongest thing on any page it appears on.\n🔴 GREEN SCRAPS: shreds of clover-green fill stuck to the muzzle, the lip corner and between two\n  front hooves - the same green as the trough, 5 to 8 scraps, present on every page from 1 to 14.\nSHEET: standing on four legs with the head buried in a trough / head flung up, ears pricked, tail\n  up / neck stretched right out with the muzzle shoved forward to point / body twisted round to\n  look at its own rump; plus three close-ups - eyes huge and mouth open in shock, eyes screwed\n  shut and muzzle wrinkled in a sulk, mouth open laughing with the eyes in half-moons. Plain\n  paper, no byre. SCENE token: CalfSpot.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - RedCow   (bake SECOND - attach the approved CalfSpot sheet so one hand draws both)\n\nFACE: a full-grown cow, broad heavy muzzle, loose folded throat, one ear that droops. Head and\n  body are ONE FLAT FILL, red-brown #A8532C, with no shading, no tint and no highlight anywhere;\n  only the ink contour and the drawn eye sit on top. The eye is a drawn ring with a dark centre\n  and a SEPARATE ink brow that lowers and gathers.\nBODY: big, deep-chested, heavy, three and a half heads long, blunt hooves, a long thick tail with\n  a heavy tuft.\n🔴 THE TAIL RULE: this animal's tail NEVER goes up. It swings SIDEWAYS across the flank at a fly,\n  a long flat S-curve, and once it swings right round and slaps something. Draw both sideways\n  states here and no upright one - the upright tail belongs to the calf until page 13.\n🔴 SCALE PLATE: the cow beside CalfSpot AT THE SAME DEPTH - the cow is twice the calf's height\n  and one cow head is nearly the calf's whole head and neck. Pages 3, 7 and 13 put them nose to\n  nose or one above the other and perspective must not flip this.\n🔴 DRY-HAY SCRAPS: pale bare-paper hay bits at this animal's lip, never green - 4 to 6 of them,\n  on every page. Green never touches this animal.\nSHEET: standing four-square chewing, jaw offset sideways / head snapped round with the muzzle\n  shoved out to point / nose to nose with a calf, neck lowered, braced / head flung right back,\n  mouth wide, laughing, belly folded / settled down on the straw with the forelegs folded under.\n  Plain paper. SCENE token: RedCow.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - ByreSet   (bake THIRD - the stage and the sound, used on every page)\n\nTHE PLACE, empty of the two leads, exactly as it sits on all fourteen pages: one LONG WOODEN\n  TROUGH down the left wall, 🔴 heaped with clover green at its LEFT END and with bare-paper dry\n  hay at its RIGHT END, the two heaps at the same depth in one vessel so the colours are compared\n  side by side. Mid-floor, one thick post and a low partition. At the right wall a SPLIT STABLE\n  DOOR with only the top half open, and through it one flat clover-green field shape with 0 marks\n  inside and a low wall as 3 strokes. On a rafter, one tin bucket. Underfoot, straw as at most 25\n  short strokes. Walls = at most 14 block outlines, 0 interior texture. Above and behind: bare\n  paper.\n🔴 THE THREE BACK-STALL COWS: cut ONE cow silhouette and repeat it THREE times in a row - same\n  outline, same size, same spacing, ink contour only, 0 patches, 0 eyes, 0 marks inside. Draw the\n  row twice: CHEWING (jaw offset to one side) and STOPPED (jaw level, page 5 only). They never\n  turn, never look and never change.\n🔴 THE WALL SOUND PLATE - draw the same stretch of wall three times:\n  A · silent: 0 arcs.\n  B · a short noise: FIVE thick ink arcs springing off the stone face and curving away from it,\n    all struck from the wall, none from the floor, none reaching into the room's middle.\n  C · a long noise: NINE arcs, longer and further apart, and the bucket doubled into two short\n    positions. 🔴 In B and C nothing marks a source - no puff, no dotted trail, no motion line at\n    any animal. Put B and C side by side and check that an arc cannot be traced back to anywhere.\n🔴 ONE FLY: a single fly as 3 ink strokes plus a short looping flight line, drawn once here at\n  actual size. It is on every page and it is always the same fly.\n0 lettering, 0 numerals, 0 signs. SCENE token: ByreSet."
