   "CHARACTER SHEET - QuiltPlate   (bake FIRST, before anything else - this book is these six lines)\n\nThe eiderdown alone on the bed, no animals, drawn four times in a row on one sheet. 🔴 SIX brush\nseam lines run its length every time; what changes is only where and how far they bow.\n\nA · EMPTY: all six seams DEAD STRAIGHT and evenly spaced, the quilt flat as a field. 🔴 This is\n  what \"nobody is in here\" looks like and it happens once, on page 1.\nB · ONE: a SMALL bow at the foot end - two seams lift a little and crowd at its sides, the other\n  four still straight.\nC · TWO: the small bow at the foot, plus a BIG ROUND bow in the middle that lifts four seams high\n  and pushes them wide apart over its crown.\nD · THREE: small, round, and a LARGEST LONG bow beside it, in that size order, the six seams now\n  bowed along their whole length with two valleys left between the humps. 🔴 The quilt spills over\n  the bed edge because there is no room left.\n🔴 CHECK ON THIS SHEET: the humps differ ONLY by how far the seams bend - not by tone, not by a\n  shadow, not by a darker edge. Cover the seams and the four states must become identical.\n🔴 ALSO DRAW, small at the bottom: a hand's width of quilt from UNDERNEATH, where the same seams\n  are crowded into dense hatching - that is how the inside of the quilt is dark, and a wedge of\n  bare paper where the hem is lifted.\nThe quilt is BARE PAPER. 0 stitches, 0 thread, 0 needle holes, 0 padding relief, 0 lettering.\nSCENE token: QuiltPlate.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - RaccoonKit   (bake SECOND)\n\nFACE: a raccoon kit, short pointed muzzle, small round ears. The mask is TWO solid ink patches\n  across the eyes with bare paper between them; each eye is a drawn ring with a dark centre and a\n  SEPARATE ink brow above it - the brows lift, knot and tilt unevenly and do all the acting.\n  Muzzle tip one small filled wedge, whiskers 3 strokes a side.\nBODY: small, round-backed, four heads long, short legs, blunt forepaws that hook and push. Body\n  fur is bare paper with 4 ink bands over the shoulders and hips, no more.\n🔴 THE TAIL: as long as the body, banded with EXACTLY 6 ink rings, and it is this book's second\n  gauge. Draw it in four states, same size: hanging straight and relaxed / dropped slack off an\n  edge / lying flat on the floor with the tip curled / held up and swinging with the tip flicked\n  sideways. The rings must be countable in every one.\nSHEET: burrowing under a quilt corner with one hind foot still out and kicking / sitting up with\n  both forepaws gripping a quilt corner and flinging it sideways / lying curled with both hind\n  legs drawn tight to the belly / hugging a bed leg with the belly flat to the wood, hind feet\n  pushing / lying flat on its belly with all four legs spread and one cheek squashed down; plus\n  three close-ups - eyes round with surprise, nose crumpled and eyes wet with grievance, eyes in\n  half-moons with the mouth open laughing. Plain paper, no room. SCENE token: RaccoonKit.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - ThreeGuests   (bake THIRD - attach RaccoonKit; the size order is the story)\n\nThree animals on one sheet, standing in a row AT THE SAME DEPTH beside RaccoonKit. 🔴 THEIR SIZE\nORDER IS FIXED AND IS WHY THE HUMPS GROW: mouse, hen, kid goat, smallest to largest.\n\nMOUSE: a mouse kit, 🔴 as big as one of the raccoon's forepaws, one closed contour, big drawn eye,\n  round ears, 4 whisker strokes, thin tail. Poses: crouched small with both forepaws at its chest,\n  blinking up / only a nose and whiskers out from under a hem, one forepaw rubbing the nose.\nHEN: a hen, two-legged as hens are, comb and wattles as small closed shapes, one drawn eye.\n  Poses: head and neck alone thrust out of a fold of quilt, beak open / 🔴 PUFFED - every feather\n  blown out so the breast is a ball and the body is WIDER THAN THE RACCOON, wings a little open,\n  eyes shut in contentment. That puffed shape is what makes the big round hump.\nKID GOAT: a young goat, 🔴 its head about twice the size of the raccoon's, two small horn buds, a\n  square muzzle, one drawn eye. Poses: crouched with the forelegs folded under, chin up, mouth\n  working / head and horns pushed out from a hem.\n🔴 SCALE PLATE: all four in one line at one depth - mouse, raccoon, puffed hen, goat - so no page\n  can flip these sizes by perspective. Plain paper, no quilt, no room. SCENE token: ThreeGuests.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - RoomSet   (bake FOURTH - the stage, on every page)\n\nThe room empty of animals and of the eiderdown, exactly as it sits on all twelve pages. Against\nthe LEFT wall, a low wooden bed in honey fill, four turned legs, 🔴 one leg with a clear flat face\na raccoon could climb; at its FOOT, a bellied tile stove drawn as an unfilled tile grid of at most\n12 squares with a small door, 0 marks inside - 🔴 nothing about the stove is ever filled, because\nit has gone out. Opposite, a small lattice window, one frame with 4 panes, showing cold-blue night\nand falling snow as at most 9 short strokes. A wide plank floor = at most 6 long lines, 0 grain,\n0 knots. Under the door, a two-finger DRAUGHT GAP as one thin cold-blue bar. One narrow board by\nthe bed is sprung a little open.\n🔴 THE COLD LADDER, drawn three times small at the bottom of the sheet: the same room with the\ncold-blue fill covering about a fifth of the frame (early), about a third (deepest, page 7), and\nNONE AT ALL with morning light and thick snow on the sill as bare paper (page 12). Only the AREA\nof the blue changes; its colour never lightens or darkens.\n🔴 One ember: a single tiny chip of honey fill inside the stove door on page 1 and nowhere else.\n0 lettering, 0 numerals. SCENE token: RoomSet."
