   "CHARACTER SHEET - FolkBands   (bake FIRST - this sheet is the entire background budget)\n\nFour repeat bands of cut folk paper, drawn as long horizontal strips on a plain plaster ground\n#F4F0E6. 🔴 EACH BAND IS ONE MOTIF REPEATED WITHOUT VARIATION - identical spacing, identical size,\n0 detail inside any single unit. They are wallpaper for the eye: the whole point is that a reader\ntakes them in once and never looks again.\n\n1 WALL FRIEZE: a cobalt #2A4C9B cut motif - a symmetrical bird-and-leaf shape - repeated AT LEAST\n  9 times along a strip, all facing the same way, 0 interior marks in any of them.\n2 SHELF FRONT: a scalloped cobalt strip with a smaller repeated cut shape, 7 units, hanging along\n  the edge of a honey shelf.\n3 APRON: one flower shape repeated over a plaster ground in cobalt, 0 shading, 0 outline drawing -\n  a printed cloth, not a painting of cloth.\n4 CROCKERY DOTS: cobalt dots on plaster-white bowls and jars, evenly spaced, 0 highlight,\n  0 reflection, 0 rim shine.\n🔴 CHECK ON THIS SHEET: cover the four bands and the page must lose no information at all. If any\n  single unit is more finished than its neighbours, or if a band is shaded or lit from one side,\n  the band has stopped being background and the book breaks.\n🔴 ALSO: one strip of the BLACK #14110E broom bristles and one small heap of the BLACK seed, side\n  by side, so the two - and only these two - are established as the only blacks in the book. All\n  drawn contours on this sheet are dark cobalt #26386E, not black.\n0 lettering, 0 numerals. SCENE token: FolkBands.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - FoxCub   (bake SECOND)\n\nFACE: a fox cub, long pointed muzzle, 🔴 BIG TRIANGULAR EARS drawn large enough to read across a\n  room - they are the acting. The head is one flat fox-red #C4552B shape with a dark cobalt\n  contour, a paler muzzle tip and a paler inner ear left as plaster. 🔴 THE EYES ARE SHUT: two\n  short curved lines with a SEPARATE cobalt brow above each; they stay shut on eleven of the\n  twelve pages and no pupil is ever drawn on those pages.\n🔴 THE EAR LADDER, same head, same size, six times in a row: BOTH FORWARD AND UPRIGHT (listening)\n  / one ear ROTATED SLOWLY toward one side while the other stays forward, the inner fur laid that\n  way / BOTH CUPPED FORWARD BY THE PAWS into funnels / HEAD TILTED far to one side so one ear\n  touches a shoulder / both flat forward and straining / both spread wide and up.\n🔴 THE TOE TALLY: one forepaw held up, drawn FOUR times with ONE, TWO, THREE and FOUR toes folded\n  in and the rest straight. This is the counter for the four right answers and must be legible at\n  a glance.\n🔴 THE OPEN EYES: draw the head ONCE more with the eyes WIDE OPEN - round dark pupils with a\n  reflection shape inside them, brows lifted. This head appears on three pages only and must look\n  like a different amount of information from every other head on the sheet.\nBODY: small, four heads tall, bushy tail, a small waistcoat. Poses: sitting on a bench with the\n  legs dangling / lying on its belly along a bench with the hind feet kicking up / creeping on\n  tiptoe with one forepaw groping sideways / standing on tiptoe with both forepaws on a\n  counter edge. Plain ground, no kitchen. SCENE token: FoxCub.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - Grandmother   (bake THIRD - attach FoxCub so both are drawn by one hand)\n\nFACE: an old fox, long muzzle, big triangular ears held relaxed and slightly back, eyes drawn as\n  narrow crescents folded at the outer corners - 🔴 she is not the one being read, so her face\n  stays simple: contour, two crescents, cobalt brows, nothing more.\nBODY: tall, broad, stooped a little at the shoulders, one flat fox-red shape. A headscarf in\n  plaster white knotted under the chin, and the FLOWERED APRON from the FolkBands sheet - the same\n  repeat, never redrawn as individual flowers.\n🔴 SCALE PLATE: grandmother beside FoxCub AT THE SAME DEPTH - she is twice the cub's height, and\n  the big mortar she works with is as wide as the cub's chest. Pages 9 and 10 put the cub, the\n  mortar and her hands in one frame and perspective must not flip these.\n🔴 FIXED GRADE: her hands work and her face barely does. She never crouches to the cub's level,\n  never gestures an explanation, never points at anything, and on pages 8 and 9 only her back,\n  shoulder and forearms are in frame with her face out of it.\nSHEET: tying an apron string behind her back with the head turned aside / one hand holding a\n  potato, the other bringing a knife down / lifting a heavy lid with one hand and stirring with\n  the other / pushing a rolling pin with both arms straight / crouched at a floor jar, twisting a\n  stiff lid / 🔴 one hand steadying a wide mortar while the other presses a wooden pestle round in\n  a circle, the shoulder rolling - this last pose is pages 6 to 9 and must be the same pose every\n  time. Plain ground. SCENE token: Grandmother.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - KitchenSet   (bake FOURTH - the stage and the five sound sources)\n\nThe kitchen empty of foxes, exactly as it sits on all twelve pages: white tile stove and long\nbench LEFT, heavy honey table centre, counter and arched brick oven RIGHT with a banded shelf of\ndotted crockery above, small window in the back wall, herb bunches as 3 flat shapes on a beam,\n🔴 A BROOM ON THE WALL BY THE DOOR - a long honey handle and a bundle of BLACK twig bristles,\nhanging untouched on every page - wooden shoes beneath it, and a lidded glass jar in the corner.\n🔴 THE FIVE SOUND SOURCES, drawn separately and in full detail, because one of them is the most\nfinished thing on each page. 🔴 THEY MUST NOT RESEMBLE ONE ANOTHER - there are no labels in this\nbook and a child tells them apart by shape alone:\n  1 BOARD: a wooden board with a potato half sliced and the cut discs laid in a row beside it, the\n    peel heaped in a bowl.\n  2 POT: a big iron pot with the lid lifted a hand's width, the surface rolling and boiling under\n    it, a splash mark on the rim.\n  3 DOUGH AND GLASS: dough rolled thin with a rolling pin, ROUND HOLES PUNCHED OUT OF IT by an\n    upturned drinking glass, and the cut discs stacked beside it.\n  4 GLASS JAR: a tall jar, its stiff lid off beside it, cucumbers under brine, the brine slopping.\n  5 MORTAR AND PESTLE: a wide earthenware bowl 🔴 WITH CLOSE-CUT GROOVES ROUND ITS INNER WALL, a\n    wooden pestle, and BLACK #14110E seed inside it - part still whole grains, part ground to a\n    glossy paste where the pestle has passed.\n🔴 THE SEED JAR: a small blue-dotted pot on the far end of the shelf, ITS LID OFF, with a few\n  BLACK grains spilled on the shelf beside it. This is the only thing in the kitchen that is not\n  plaster, cobalt, red or honey, and it is planted on page 1.\n🔴 Firelight = ONE flat fox-red shape at the oven mouth with a hard cut edge. 0 beams, 0 glow.\n0 lettering, 0 numerals. SCENE token: KitchenSet."
